        </p>
        </aside>
        <p>
          <alert>Robert Alan Styer</alert>

          <!--   earned his BA (double major in mathematics and physics, Cumma Sum Laude) from the -->
          <!--   University of Pennsylvania in <m>1977</m>, and his Ph.D. from the -->
          <!--   Massachusetts Institute of Technology in <m>1981</m>. -->
          <!-- </p> -->
          <!-- <p> -->
          <!--   Robert has been an active member of EPaDel since <m>1999.</m> -->
          <!-- </p> -->
          graduated summa cum laude with a degree in mathematics and physics
          from the University of Pennsylvania, and he earned his PhD from
          M.I.T. with a dissertation in analytic number theory under Harold
          Stark. In 1983, after a stint as the Lawton Lecturer at Temple
          University, Styer joined the faculty of Villanova University, where he
          taught a wide range of courses and held many positions in university
          leadership, including Chair of the Department of Mathematics Sciences
          (later the Department of Mathematics and Statistics) (1997-2003),
          Chair of the University Senate (2008-2014). He has been the Director
          of the Comprehensive Science program since 2011 and expects to remain
          in that position until about 2026.
        </p>
<p>
Styer served as EPADEL President (2007-2009), multiple terms as an
At-Large member of the Executive Committee, and was a local organizer
for section meetings in spring of 1999, fall of 2005, and fall of
2016. He has been active in promoting mathematics and science in the
Philadelphia area, having taught a course on the Elementary and Middle
School Math Olympiads, coordinated the MAA AMC/AIME exams for
homeschoolers and was involved with two NSF Education grants to assist
teachers with STEM education training.
</p>
</li>
    <li xml:id="MaranoBio">
