    <li xml:id="BomanBio">
      <aside>
        <p>
          Ph.D. Adviser: Israel Koltracht
        </p>
        <p>
          Dissertation Title: <pubtitle> Fast Algorithms for Toeplitz Equations</pubtitle>
        </p>
        </aside>
        <p> 
          <alert>
            Eugene (Bud) Boman 
          </alert> 

          earned his BA in Mathematics from Reed College in 1984, his MA
          and PhD from the University of Connecticut in 1986 and 1993,
          respectively. In 1996 he became an Assistant Professor at the
          DuBois campus of Penn State and joined the Allegheny Mountain
          Section of the MAA where he served in various leadership roles
          between 1996 and 2006 when he moved to Penn State’s Harrisburg
          campus (Capital College). Because Penn State campuses are
          split between two MAA sections this move made him a member of
          the EPADEL section, where he remained for the duration of his
          career.
        </p>
        <p>
          He served on the EPADEL Executive Board as the book table coordinator,
          as a Member-At-Large, Vice Chair, and Chair. He was appointed the
          first Section Historian when that position was created in 2011.
        </p>
        <p>
          Bud was one of the 2008 recipients of the MAA’s Carl B. Allendorfer
          Award for his paper, <pubtitle><url href="https://maa.org/carl-b-allendoerfer-awards/" visual="maa.org/carl-b-allendoerfer-awards">Mom! There's an Astroid in My Closet!</url></pubtitle>
          (Mathematics Magazine, vol. 80, (2007), pp. 104-111)
        </p> 
    </li>
    <li xml:id="CatepillanBio">
      <aside>
        <p>
          Ph.D. Adviser: Raúl Curto
        </p>
        <p>
          Dissertation Title: <pubtitle>Canonically/Invertible Koszul and Quasinormal Pairs of Operators on Hilbert Space</pubtitle>
        </p>
        </aside>      
        <p>
          <alert>Ximena Catepillán </alert>
          
          is from the town of Corral, Chile and taught at Universidad
          de Magallanes by the Strait of Magellan in the Chilean
          Patagonia before coming to the US.  She earned her both her
          MA (1989) and PhD (1991) in Mathematics from the University of Iowa.
        </p>
        <p>
          During her <m>26</m> years as a member of EPADEL (as of <m>2023</m>)
          Ximena has served as a Member-At-Large (<m>2007-2012</m> of the
          Executive Council, as Vice-Chair (<m>2015-2017</m>), and as Chair (<m>2017-2019</m>).
        </p>
        <p>
          In <m>2011</m> she created that Silent Book Auction to help replace the loss of funds that resulted when the section's book sale was discontinued.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ximena has contributed a chapter to each of <pubtitle>
          Mathematical Themes in a First-Year Seminar
          </pubtitle> (<m>2021</m>) and <pubtitle>
          Cross-Curricular Pure Math Applications
          </pubtitle> (<m>2022</m>). With Linda McGuire she also contributed a chapter to the book <pubtitle>
          Count Me In, Community and Belonging in Mathematics
          </pubtitle> (<m>2022</m>), and she began serving as the Chair of the MAA Special Interest Group on the History of Mathematics (SIGHOM).
        </p>
<p>
  In 2025 Ximena won the MAA<rsq/>s 
  <url href="https://maa.org/yueh-gin-gung-and-dr-charles-y-hu-award-for-distinguished-service/" visual="maa.org/yueh-gin-gung-and-dr-charles-y-hu-award-for-distinguished-service/">Yueh<ndash/>Gin Gung and Dr. Charles Y. Hu Award for Distinguished Service</url>.
</p>


    </li>
    <li xml:id="PanofskyBio">
      <aside>
        <p>
          Ph.D. Adviser: Garth Isaak
        </p>
        <p>
          Dissertation Title: <pubtitle>Graph Labeling Problems with Distance Conditions</pubtitle>
        </p>
        </aside>
        <p>
          <alert>
            Ellen Panofsky
          </alert>
          
          was raised outside of Philadelphia, PA (specifically in the
          towns of Bensalem and Holland). She had to switch schools in
          her very early years and because of inconsistencies in the
          schedules of the schools she learned to add and subtract
          before she learned to read.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ellen earned her B.S. Ed. from Millersville University in 2002,
          her M.S. from Lehigh University in 2004, and her Ph.D. also
          from Lehigh in 2008. From 2008 to 2012 she was an Assistant
          Professor at Cabrini College. In 2012 she accepted an
          Assistant Professor of Instruction position at Temple
          University. She was promoted to Associate Professor of
          Instruction in 2018.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ellen served on the EPADEL Executive Board as a
          Member<ndash/>At<ndash/>Large (2011<ndash/>2017), Vice Chair
          (2018<ndash/>2019), and Chair (2020<ndash/>2021). Her
          predecessor as both Chair and Vice Chair was her former
          professor at Millersville, <xref ref="CatepillanBio"
          text="custom">Ximena Catepillan</xref>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ellen has the dubious and unsought distinction of being the
          only Chair or President of the section never to have stood
          in front of the assembled membership to conduct a meeting.
          She became chair just after to the onset of the COVID-19
          pandemic and unfortunately, the pandemic coincided almost
          exactly with Ellen<rsq/>s time as Chair. While she was never able
          to preside over a physical meeting of the section she was
          able to shepherd the section through that difficult time by
          organizing virtual meetings.
        </p>
    </li>
    <li xml:id="DromsBio">
      <aside>
        <p>
          Ph.D. Adviser:  Thomas E. Mark
        </p>
        <p>
          Dissertation Title: Constructions of Stein Fillings
        </p>
        </aside>
        <p>
          <alert>Sean Droms </alert>

          was born near Harrisonburg, Virginia. He earned his BS in 2007 from the
          University of Mary Washington, in Fredericksburg, Virginia, and his
          Ph.D. in 2013 from the University of Virginia, in Charlottesville, Virginia.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sean joined the math faculty at Lebanon Valley College, in
          Annville, Pennsylvania in 2013 and was promoted to Associate
          Professor in 2019.
        </p>
        <p>
          He has been a member of the section since 2013, serving as a
          Member-At-Large of the Executive Committee from 2015 to
          2018, as Section Historian from 2016 to 2019, as webmaster
          from 2019 to 2023. He served as the Vice Chair in 2020 and
          2021, as as chair of the Executive Committee from 2021 to
          2023.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
