<p>
<ul>
    <li xml:id="FraboniBio">
      <aside>
        <p>
          Advisor: Terrence John Napier
        </p>
        <p>
          Dissertation Title: Some <m>q</m>-Convexity Properties of Coverings of Convex Manifolds 
        </p>
      </aside>
      <p>
        <alert>Michael Fraboni </alert>
        was born in Waverly, New York. He earned a BS in Mathematics at
        he University of Scranton in 1997, his MS and Ph. D. from Lehigh
        University in 1999 and 2002, respectively.
</p>
<p>
  Michael has been a member of EPaDel since his undergraduate
  days.  While at Scranton he won the EPaDel student paper
  competition in 1997 for a paper he wrote about work he did
  with Prof. Ken Monks.
</p>
<p>
  He served as the section's newsletter editor from 2003 to
  2006. When the editor position was combined with the newly
  created webmaster position in 2006 he continued to serve as
  both until 2012.
</p>
<p>
  in 2002 Michael was hired into the faculty Moravian College in
  Bethlehem, Pennsylvania. He was promoted to Associate
  Professor in 2008, and Professor in 20018.
</p>
    </li>
    <li xml:id="MollerBio">
      <aside>
        <p>
          Advisor: Garth Timothy Isaak
        </p>
        <p>
          Dissertation Title: <m>t</m>-Split Interval Orders
        </p>
        </aside>
        <p>
          <alert>Trisha Moller </alert>
          was born in Rahway, NJ, and for most of her youth, she aspired
          to become an artist. After tutoring her best friend in high
          school, mathematics won her over, but she has maintained love
          for both mathematics and art for her whole career. Trisha
          earned a BS in Mathematics and Secondary Education from
          Trenton State College (now The College of NJ) in 1997, and
          then worked for AT&amp;T for a year before enrolling in the
          graduate program at Lehigh University.  In 2004 Trisha earned
          her PhD in Mathematics from Lehigh University with a
          dissertation in graph theory under the guidance of Garth
          Isaak. It was in graduate school at Lehigh University that
          Trisha met and married fellow mathematician, Mike
          Fraboni. After a start on the tenure track at DeSales
          University, Trisha transitioned to full-time mom and part-time
          professor with the birth of her two sons. Trisha has held many
          positions on the EPADEL executive committee such as Section
          NExT coordinator, chair of the Committee on the Session for
          New Colleagues, Student Speaker Coordinator, Public
          Information Officer, and organizer of the 2005 EPADEL Student
          Career Conference. Trisha is a 2004 MAA Project NExT fellow,
          and in 2011, she earned the AAUW Bethlehem Emerging Star
          Award. In 2021, the Calypso Elementary Dr. Trisha Moller
          Leadership Award was established in her honor.
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>
